  "answer_text": "Here is a comprehensive breakdown of the word **INDOLENT**:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcd6 Definition\n\n**Indolent** means *averse to activity, effort, or movement* \u2014 in other words, **habitually lazy**.\n\nIt is defined as *having or showing a disposition to avoid exertion; slothful.*\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd24 Part of Speech\n**Indolent** is an **adjective** meaning slow or lazy.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udca1 Key Nuances\n\n- **General use:** An indolent person is slow and lazy \u2014 not the type of person you'd want running a corporation or competing in a relay race.\n\n- **Medical use:** Doctors also use the word *indolent* to describe medical conditions that are **slow to progress**. If diagnosed with an illness, you'd prefer an indolent one over one that spreads quickly.\n\n- **In pathology:** In pathology, *indolent* can mean *causing little pain*, or (especially of a painless ulcer) **slow to heal**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf31 Etymology (Word Origin)\n\nThe word comes from **Late Latin** *indolent-, indolens*, meaning *insensitive to pain*, from Latin *in-* + *dolent-, dolens*, the present participle of *dol\u0113re*, meaning \"to feel pain.\"\n\nThe main modern sense of \"laziness, love of ease\" dates to around **1710**, and perhaps reflects the notion of avoiding trouble.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd01 Synonyms\nSynonyms for *indolent* include: **lazy, slack, idle, slow, sluggish, inactive, inert, languid, lethargic, listless, lackadaisical, torpid, slothful.**\n\n---\n\n### \u270f\ufe0f Example Sentences\n- *\"The indolent employee spent more time chatting than working.\"*\n- *\"Despite the pressing deadline, he maintained an indolent pace, unconcerned about the consequences.\"*\n\nIn short, **indolent** is a formal or literary way of calling someone (or something) **lazy, slow, and disinclined to put in effort**.",
